	RVM

RVM is a RISC-V system emulator for 64 bit machines. It implements RV64IMAFDC,
the Sv39, Sv48 and Sv57 page tables, and enough of virtio to boot a stock Debian
kernel and userland from a disk image. It runs on Linux and macOS, and there is
an Android app under android/.

It is C11 against libc and libm. Nothing else is needed to build it.


Building
--------

	make

gives you ./rvm, about 100 KB. A C11 compiler and GNU make are enough.

	make test

builds and runs the test suite, currently 34 suites and 677 assertions.

	make lint
	make MODE=sanity test
	make format

are the -Werror syntax pass, an ASan and UBSan build, and clang-format.
docs/BUILDING has every target, mode and variable.


Running it
----------

Every GitHub release has a kernel, an initrd and a disk image attached, so
downloading those is the shortest way. To build them yourself:

	./tools/fetch_kernel.sh --out vmlinux
	sudo ./tools/mkrootfs.sh --out disk.img
	./tools/run.sh

The boot ends at a login prompt on ttyS0. mkrootfs.sh needs root, around 2 GB of
free memory and a route to deb.debian.org.

No DTB file is required: rvm builds its device tree at boot. dtb/rvm.dts is the
same tree written out for editing, and make dtb compiles it if you want to
compare the two.

	./rvm --help

prints the options. The useful ones are -m for guest memory, -k for the kernel,
-d for the disk, --stats for counters and --trace-from for the instruction
trace.


Documentation
-------------

	PLAN			what is built, what is not, and the rules
	CHANGELOG		what changed
	CONTRIBUTING		how to send a patch
	SECURITY		how to report a guest escape
	docs/ARCHITECTURE	how the pieces fit together
	docs/BUILDING		build modes, targets, the APK, CI
	docs/DEBUGGING		tracing a guest that goes wrong


Android
-------

The app lives in android/. It is one Activity and three custom Views. There is
no AndroidX, no Material Components, no Compose and no WebView: the terminal is
drawn on a Canvas and the graphics output goes to a SurfaceView. The
dependencies block in android/app/build.gradle.kts is empty and should stay
that way.

android/jni/CMakeLists.txt compiles src/ directly, so there is one copy of the
emulator and the desktop build cannot drift away from the phone build.

	cd android && gradle --no-daemon assembleDebug

There is no gradle wrapper jar in this repository, so ./gradlew does not exist.
Use your own Gradle 8.11.1, or let CI do the build. The release APK is around
150 KB, and the workflow fails if it ever goes over 5 MB.


Booting on a phone
------------------

The default kernel cmdline is

	console=ttyS0 earlycon=ns16550a,mmio32,0x10000000 root=/dev/vda rootwait rw

with no init= anywhere. What decides whether you get a shell is the initrd:
tools/mkinitrd.sh builds a busybox initramfs whose /init brings eth0 up through
the built-in NAT and then, if /dev/vda has an /sbin/init, hands the console over
to it so you land in the distro. Putting rvm.shell=1 on --bootargs keeps the
initramfs shell instead and skips the hand-off.

The APK ships that same initramfs as an asset, so on a phone Kernel and Disk are
the only two files you have to pick.

Two more bootargs matter there, and the app passes both. rvm.time=virtual makes
mtime follow retired instructions instead of the host wall clock (see
src/devices/clint.c), so a boot that is slow because the host is slow is not
also a guest that believes its CPU is stuck. nosoftlockup silences the kernel
watchdog, which on a device with a foreground app and a two gigabyte copy in
progress tends to report true things in the wrong order.

The app also has a machine line: menu -> Machine edits ram=1536 cpu=1, clamped
to 256-4096 MiB and remembered between runs. After Boot the console prints the
sizes the VM really got.

While a VM is running the app holds a PARTIAL_WAKE_LOCK. Without it Android
stops scheduling the vCPU thread when the screen times out, and a boot that
needs tens of minutes of CPU on a phone simply halts mid-init without saying
anything.

"Boot to shell" is a branch inside the initramfs /init, not something the
emulator implements, so an initrd copied out of an older release accepts the
flag and ignores it. The app reads the image it is about to boot, puts its own
copy in place when that image predates the feature, and prints the boot
arguments and the initrd's capability in the banner. That banner is what tells
you which of the two ways the setting did nothing.

Adding shell=1 to the machine line skips the disk's init scripts and hands the
console to its /bin/sh instead (the initramfs calls this rvm.shell=root). On a
phone that is the difference between a prompt in ten seconds and a silent minute
or two, which is enough to run apt, ping and dpkg but not to have any service
running.


Continuous integration
----------------------

build, test and android run on every push to main and on pull requests. kernel,
rootfs and release run on a v* tag: they build the kernel, the initramfs, the
Debian disk and the APK, and attach all of it to the GitHub release. There are
no status badges; look at the Actions tab.


